daptive, funding of climate impact, funding of climate impacts, funding of climate risk, funding of climate risks, funding of climate-proofing, funding of improving resilience, funding of reducing climate risk, funding of reducing climate risks, funding of resilience, funding of resilient, funding of risk, funds for adaptation, funds for adaptive, funds for climate impact, funds for climate impacts, funds for climate risk, funds for climate risks, funds for climate-proofing, funds for improving resilience, funds for reducing climate risk, funds for reducing climate risks, funds for resilience, funds for resilient, funds for risk, investing in adaptation, investing in adaptive, investing in climate impact, investing in climate impacts, investing in climate risk, investing in climate risks, investing in climate-proofing, investing in improving resilience, investing in reducing climate risk, investing in reducing climate risks, investing in resilience, investing in resilient, investing in risk, money for adaptation, money for adaptive, money for climate impact, money for climate impacts, money for climate risk, money for climate risks, money for climate-proofing, money for improving resilience, money for reducing climate risk, money for reducing climate risks, money for resilience, money for resilient, money for risk, paying for adaptation, paying for adaptive, paying for climate impact, paying for climate impacts, paying for climate risk, paying for climate risks, paying for climate-proofing, paying for improving resilience, paying for reducing climate risk, paying for reducing climate risks, paying for resilience, paying for resilient, paying for risk, resources for adaptation, resources for adaptive, resources for climate impact, resources for climate impacts, resources for climate risk, resources for climate risks, resources for climate-proofing, resources for improving resilience, resources for reducing climate risk, resources for reducing climate risks, resources for resilience, resources for resilient, resources for risk, adaptation capital, adaptation financial, adaptation funds, adaptation investments, adaptation payments, climate risk capital, climate risk finance, climate risk financial, climate risk financing, climate risk funding, climate risk funds, climate risk investment, climate risk investments, climate risk payments, disaster risk capital, disaster risk finance, disaster risk financial, disaster risk financing, disaster risk funding, disaster risk funds, disaster risk investment, disaster risk investments, disaster risk payments, resilience capital, resilience finance, resilience financial, resilience financing, resilience funding, resilience funds, resilience investment, resilience investments, resilience payments, risk reduction capital, risk reduction finance, risk reduction financial, risk reduction financing, risk reduction funding, risk reduction funds, risk reduction investment, risk reduction investments, risk reduction payments</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P20 xml:lang="en">r4qxcp34</cpr:P20>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1585"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Climate finance encompasses financial actors and resources primarily directed towards climate change mitigation, adaptation and compensation. It is characterised by its specific intent to address the challenges and impacts of climate change. Adaptation finance is specifically allocated to activities that enhance resilience to climate impacts.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Finance that is primarily directed towards climate change adaptation, including increasing resilience and robustness to actual or expected climate change and its effects.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1346 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1346">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1343"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">loss and damage finance</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">finance for loss and damage, l&amp;d finance, finance for addressing loss &amp; damage, finance for addressing loss and damage, finance for loss &amp; damage, financing for addressing loss &amp; damage, financing for addressing loss and damage, financing loss &amp; damage, financing loss and damage, funding for addressing loss &amp; damage, funding for addressing loss and damage, funding for loss &amp; damage, funding for loss and damage, l &amp; d finance, l &amp; d funding, l&amp;d funding, loss &amp; damage funding, loss and damage funding, l &amp; d investment, l&amp;d investment, loss and damage investment, finance for l&amp;d, financing l&amp;d</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P20 xml:lang="en">aqrcdtxx</cpr:P20>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Climate finance encompasses financial actors and resources primarily directed towards climate change mitigation, adaptation and compensation. It is characterised by its specific intent to address the challenges and impacts of climate change. Loss and damage finance refers to financial mechanisms that cover the significant and often unavoidable economic costs from climate change impacts that remain even after the most effective adaptation measures are applied, particularly in vulnerable regions, as illustrated by integrated economic models that compare optimal adaptation spending with the persistent residual damages.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Funding provided to help pay for the economic harm from climate change that cannot be completely prevented by adaptation measures.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q135 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q135">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1846"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">ecological buffer zones</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">ecological buffer zone, ecological buffers, ecological buffer, biodiversity buffer zone, biodiversity buffer zones</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Buffer zones are designated areas used to protect sensitive landscape patches (e.g., wetlands, wildlife reserves) to lessen the impacts of human activity and land disturbance.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Buffer zones are designated areas used to protect sensitive landscape patches (e.g., wetlands, wildlife reserves) to lessen the impacts of human activity and land disturbance.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q136 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q136">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q136"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1846"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">wildfire control</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">bushfire reduction, firebreaks, fighting wildfires, wildfire containment, wildfire suppression, create a fire break, bushfire containment, bushfire suppression, containing bushfires, containing wildfires, controlling bushfires, controlling wildfires, suppressing bushfires, suppressing wildfires, fire breaks</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q137"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q140"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q422"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Wildfire control refers to efforts and strategies aimed at minimizing the risk, frequency, and severity of wildfires. One strategy is the &apos;firebreaks&apos;, which purpose is to prevent forest fires from spreading before they damage the ecosystem, cropland or personal property. It consists in creating cleared areas to stop or slow the spread of wildfires.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Wildfire control refers to efforts and strategies aimed at minimizing the risk, frequency, and severity of wildfires. One strategy is the &apos;firebreaks&apos;, which purpose is to prevent forest fires from spreading before they damage the ecosystem, cropland or personal property. It consists in creating cleared areas to stop or slow the spread of wildfires.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1362 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1362">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1343"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">climate fund</rdfs:label>
        <cpr:P20 xml:lang="en">bugd7dbt</cpr:P20>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">A dedicated financial mechanism that mobilises, manages and disburses resources to support actions addressing climate change. Climate funds are designed to facilitate the transition to a low-carbon, climate-resilient future by financing projects and initiatives that reduce greenhouse gas emissions (mitigation), help societies adapt to the impacts of climate change (adaptation) and pay for the economic harm from climate change that cannot be completely prevented by adaptation measures (loss and damage). Climate funds can be public, private or a blend of both but this concept is focused on public climate finance. They may operate at international, national or local levels but this concept is focused on international and national climate finance flows. Climate funds use a range of financial instruments and are often structured to attract additional investment from governments, development banks, businesses and other stakeholders. Their governance, priorities and funding mechanisms vary, but all climate funds share the common goal of accelerating climate action through strategic financial support.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Dedicated pool of financial resources for climate-related projects.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1363 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1363">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1362"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">multilateral climate fund</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">MCFs, Accelerating Coal Transition Program, Adaptation Fund, Clean Technology Fund, Climate Investment Fund, Climate Investment Funds, CTF, GCF, GEF, Global Environment Facility, Green Climate Fund, LDCF, Least Developed Countries Fund, L&amp;D Fund, Loss &amp; Damage Fund, Scaling Up Renewable Energy Program for Low Income Countries, SCCF, SCF, Special Climate Change Fund, SREP, Strategic Climate Fund, Climate Smart Cities Program, Forest Investment Program, Global Climate Action Program, Industry Decarbonization Program, Loss and Damage Fund, Nature, People and Climate Investment Program, Pilot Program for Climate Resilience, Renewable Energy Integration Program, Scaling up Renewable Energy Program in Low Income Countries, L &amp; D Fund, Accelerating Coal Transition</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1361"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1364"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1583"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">A financial mechanism that pools resources from multiple donor countries and institutions to support climate change mitigation and adaptation projects across developing nations. Typically managed by international organisations or development banks, a multilateral climate fund operates with established governance structures and funding criteria.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">International institution funded by developed countries to distribute climate grants and/or loans.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1364 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1364">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1362"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">accredited entity</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">implementing agency</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1363"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">An organisation that has been formally approved by a climate fund to receive and manage climate finance resources on behalf of implementing partners. These entities must meet specific fiduciary standards, environmental and social safeguards, and demonstrate capacity to develop and implement climate projects. Accredited entities can be national, regional, or international organisations that serve as intermediaries between climate funds and project executors and can be accredited as implementing, delivery or executing entities.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">An organisation accredited to partner with climate funds to implement projects.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1365 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1365">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1362"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">bilateral climate fund or initiative</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">GCCI, GCPF, Global Climate Change Initiative, Global Climate Partnership Fund, IKI, International Climate Forest Initiative, International Climate Fund, International Climate Initiative, Internationale Klimaschutzinitiative, MDG Achievement Fund, MDG-F, NAMA Facility, Nationally Appropriate Mitigation Action Facility, NICFI, Norway&apos;s International Climate Forest Initiative, REDD+ Early Movers, REM, Africa Renewable Energy Initiative, AREI, GEEREF, Global Energy Efficiency and Renewable Energy Fund</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">A climate financing arrangement where one country directly provides climate-related funding to another country or specific projects. These initiatives are typically managed through the donor country&apos;s development agency or dedicated climate programs and reflect the donor&apos;s strategic priorities and interests. Bilateral climate funds often focus on specific regions or types of climate action that align with the donor country&apos;s foreign policy objectives.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Climate fund or project undertaken through a partnership between two countries.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1366 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1366">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1362"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">national climate fund</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Amazon Fund, Bangladesh Climate Change Resilience Fund, Bangladesh Climate Change Trust Fund, Benin National Fund for the Environment and Climate, Brazilian National Fund on Climate Change, Cambodia Climate Change Alliance Trust Fund, Climate Resilient Green Economy, Climate Resilient Green Economy - Ethiopia, CVF &amp; V20 Joint Multi Donor Fund, CVF &amp; V20 Joint Multi-Donor Fund, CVF and V20 Joint Multi Donor Fund, CVF and V20 Joint Multi-Donor Fund, Guyana REDD+ Investment Fund, Indonesia Climate Change Trust Fund, JETP, JETPs, Just Energy Transition Partnership, Just Energy Transition Partnerships, Maldives Climate Change Trust Fund, Mali Climate Fund, Mexico Climate Change Fund, Philippines People&apos;s Survival Fund, Rwanda Green Fund, Rwanda&apos;s Green Fund, South Africa Green Fund, Fundo Amazônia</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">A country-specific funding mechanism established to receive and manage climate finance from both domestic and international sources. These funds serve as national entities that coordinate climate-related investments, ensure alignment with national climate strategies and streamline the implementation of climate projects within the country. They often act as central coordinating bodies for climate finance at the national level.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Fund established by an individual country to meet national climate goals.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1367 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1367">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">public finance actor</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">public financial institution, state-backed financial body</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">These actors are usually majority-owned, backed or significantly controlled by nation-state governments. Their connection to governmental structures shapes their operation within the climate finance landscape. Their activities are guided by a public mandate and they can deploy a range of financial instruments including grants, loans, equity and guarantees. Public finance actors may operate at a national or international level, and may collaborate with other public and private entities to influence climate action. They can play pivotal roles by directing public resources towards climate projects and by aligning activities with national priorities, but not all are engaged in climate finance activities.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">An enterprise or financial institution that is usually majority-owned, backed or significantly controlled by nation-state governments.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1368 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1368">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1367"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">export credit agency</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Export-Import Bank of the United States, Japan Bank for International Cooperation, JBIC, Atradius Dutch State Business, Bpifrance Assurance Export, Eksportkreditt Norge, Euler Hermes Aktiengesellschaft, Export Credit Norway, Export Development Canada, Export Finance Australia, Export-Import Bank of China, Export-Import Bank of India, Finnvera plc, SACE S.p.A., Servizi Assicurativi del Commercio Estero, Swedish Export Credit Corporation, UK Export Finance, UKEF, US Export-Import Bank, Afreximbank, African Export-Import Bank, African Trade Insurance Agency, Agência Crédito à Exportação do Brasil S.A., ASHRA, Atradius, AuslandsGeschäftsAbsicherung der Bundesrepublik Deutschland, Banco de Comercio Exterior de Colombia, Banco Nacional de Comercio Exterior, Bancóldex, Bancomext, BPI FAE, BPIfrance Assurance Export, Brazilian Guarantees Agency, CAGEX, China Export &amp; Credit Insurance Corporation, CoBank, Coface, Compagnie Algérienne Assurance et de Garantie des Exportations, Companhia de Seguro de Créditos, Compañía Española de Seguros de Crédito a la Exportación CESCE, Credendo, Czech Export Bank, Denmark&apos;s Export and Investment Fund, ECGC Limited, ECGE, ECIO, EGAP, EGFI, EICIA, EIFO, EKN, Eksfin, Etihad Credit Insurance, Ex-Im Bank, EXIM Brasil, Eximbank, Eximbank SR, Export and Investment Fund of Denmark, Export Credit Bank of Turkey, Export Credit Guarantee Company of Egypt, Export Credit Insurance Organisation, Export Credit Office, Export Credits Agency, Export Finance and Insurance Corporation, Export Finance Norway, Export Guarantee and Insurance Corporation, Export Guarantee Fund of Iran, Export Import Bank of Bangladesh Limited, Export Insurance Agency of Russia, Export-Import Bank of the Slovak Republic, Export-Import Credit Insurance Agency, Export–Import Bank of the Republic of China, Exportkreditnämnden, Finnish Export Credit Ltd, Finnvera, Foreign Insurance Agency of Russia, GIEK, Hong Kong Export Credit Insurance Corporation, Hungarian Export Credit Insurance Ltd, Hungarian Export-Import Bank, ICIEC, Islamic Corporation for the Insurance of Investment and Export Credit, Israel Foreign Trade Risks Insurance Corporation, JLGC, Jordan Loan Guarantee Cooperation, K-SURE, KEXIM, Korea Trade Insurance Corporation, Korporacja Ubezpieczeń Kredytów Eksportowych, Kredex Krediidikindlustus, KUKE, KUKE S.A., Loan Guarantee &amp; Export Credit Guarantee, MEHIB, Ministerio de Economía, NEXI, Nigerian Export-Import Bank, Nippon Export and Investment Insurance, OeKB, Oesterreichische Kontrollbank AG, Office du Ducroire, Philippine Guarantee Corporation, SACE S.p.A. Servizi Assicurativi del Commercio Estero, Saudi Export Program, Sinosure, SLECIC, Société Marocaine d&apos;Assurance à l&apos;Exportation, Sri Lanka Export Credit Insurance Corporation, Swiss Export Risk Insurance, The EXIM Bank of Pakistan, The Export-Import Bank of Korea, The Norwegian Guarantee Institute for Export Credits, Türk Eximbank, United Kingdom Export Finance</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P20 xml:lang="en">9n9xsymu</cpr:P20>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Government-backed institution that offers financing, guarantees, and insurance to support domestic companies’ international exports—including funding climate‐related projects by reducing commercial and political risks.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">An agency providing government-backed financing, guarantees, and insurance to support domestic companies&apos; international exports, that can play a role in financing climate-related projects.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1369 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1369">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1367"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">central bank</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Bank of Canada, Bank of England, Bank of Japan, Bank of Korea, Central Bank of Brazil, Deutsche Bundesbank, European Central Bank, Federal Reserve, People&apos;s Bank of China, Reserve Bank of Australia, Reserve Bank of New Zealand, Sveriges Riksbank, Swiss National Bank, Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas, Bangladesh Bank, Bank Al-Maghrib, Bank Indonesia, Bank of Albania, Bank of Algeria, Bank of Botswana, Bank of Cape Verde, Bank of Central African States, Bank of Eritrea, Bank of Estonia, Bank of Finland, Bank of France, Bank of Ghana, Bank of Greece, Bank of Guatemala, Bank of Guyana, Bank of Israel, Bank of Italy, Bank of Jamaica, Bank of Latvia, Bank of Lithuania, Bank of Mauritius, Bank of Mexico, Bank of Mongolia, Bank of Mozambique, Bank of Namibia, Bank of Papua New Guinea, Bank of Portugal, Bank of Sierra Leone, Bank of Slovenia, Bank of Somaliland, Bank of South Sudan, Bank of Spain, Bank of Tanzania, Bank of Thailand, Bank of the Lao People&apos;s Democratic Republic, Bank of the Republic, Bank of the Republic of Burundi, Bank of the Republic of Haiti, Bank of Uganda, Bank of Zambia, Banque du Liban, Bermuda Monetary Authority, Brunei Darussalam Central Bank, Bulgarian National Bank, Cayman Islands Monetary Authority, Central Bank of Argentina, Central Bank of Armenia, Central Bank of Aruba, Central Bank of Azerbaijan, Central Bank of Bahrain, Central Bank of Barbados, Central Bank of Belize, Central Bank of Bolivia, Central Bank of Bosnia and Herzegovina, Central Bank of Chile, Central Bank of Costa Rica, Central Bank of Cuba, Central Bank of Curaçao and Sint Maarten, Central Bank of Cyprus, Central Bank of Djibouti, Central Bank of Ecuador, Central Bank of Egypt, Central Bank of Eswatini, Central Bank of Honduras, Central Bank of Iceland, Central Bank of Iraq, Central Bank of Ireland, Central Bank of Jordan, Central Bank of Kenya, Central Bank of Kosovo, Central Bank of Kuwait, Central Bank of Lesotho, Central Bank of Liberia, Central Bank of Libya, Central Bank of Luxembourg, Central Bank of Madagascar, Central Bank of Malaysia, Central Bank of Malta, Central Bank of Mauritania, Central Bank of Montenegro, Central Bank of Myanmar, Central Bank of Nicaragua, Central Bank of Nigeria, Central Bank of Oman, Central Bank of Paraguay, Central Bank of Russia, Central Bank of Samoa, Central Bank of São Tomé and Príncipe, Central Bank of Seychelles, Central Bank of Solomon Islands, Central Bank of Somalia, Central Bank of Sri Lanka, Central Bank of Sudan, Central Bank of Suriname, Central Bank of Syria, Central Bank of The Bahamas, Central Bank of the Comoros, Central Bank of the Congo, Central Bank of the Democratic People&apos;s Republic of Korea, Central Bank of the Dominican Republic, Central Bank of The Gambia, Central Bank of the Islamic Republic of Iran, Central Bank of the Republic of China (Taiwan), Central Bank of the Republic of Guinea, Central Bank of the Republic of San Marino, Central Bank of the Republic of Turkey, Central Bank of the Republic of Uzbekistan, Central Bank of the Turkish Republic of Northern Cyprus, Central Bank of the United Arab Emirates, Central Bank of Trinidad and Tobago, Central Bank of Tunisia, Central Bank of Turkmenistan, Central Bank of Uruguay, Central Bank of Venezuela, Central Bank of West African States, Central Bank of Yemen, Central Reserve Bank of El Salvador, Central Reserve Bank of Peru, Croatian National Bank, Czech National Bank, Da Afghanistan Bank, Danmarks Nationalbank, De Nederlandsche Bank, East Timor Central Bank, Eastern Caribbean Central Bank, Hong Kong Monetary Authority, Hungarian National Bank, Maldives Monetary Authority, Monetary Authority of Macao, Monetary Authority of Singapore, National Bank of Angola, National Bank of Belgium, National Bank of Cambodia, National Bank of Ethiopia, National Bank of Georgia, National Bank of Kazakhstan, National Bank of Liechtenstein, National Bank of Moldova, National Bank of North Macedonia, National Bank of Poland, National Bank of Romania, National Bank of Rwanda, National Bank of Serbia, National Bank of Slovakia, National Bank of Tajikistan, National Bank of the Kyrgyz Republic, National Bank of the Republic of Abkhazia, National Bank of the Republic of Belarus, National Bank of the Republic of South Ossetia, National Bank of Ukraine, National Reserve Bank of Tonga, Nepal Rastra Bank, Norges Bank, Overseas Issuing Institute, Palestine Monetary Authority, Qatar Central Bank, Reserve Bank of Fiji, Reserve Bank of India, Reserve Bank of Malawi, Reserve Bank of Vanuatu, Reserve Bank of Zimbabwe, Royal Monetary Authority of Bhutan, Saudi Central Bank, South African Reserve Bank, State Bank of Pakistan, State Bank of Vietnam, Transnistrian Republican Bank</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P20 xml:lang="en">wyaugrjm</cpr:P20>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">National institution that manages a country’s currency and monetary policy, and in some jurisdictions increasingly incorporates green monetary strategies to mitigate climate risks.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">A financial institution that manages a country&apos;s currency and can promote climate-related initiatives through green monetary policy and other means.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q137 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q137">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1846"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">prescribed fire</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">prescribed fire, prescribed burning</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q136"/>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q140"/>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Prescribed fire is a form of land management in which fire is intentionally applied to vegetation. Prescribed fires are conducted under desired conditions to meet specific objectives, such as to restore fire regimes in adapted ecosystems or to limit the amount of dry brush in an area prone to wildfires.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Prescribed fire is a form of land management in which fire is intentionally applied to vegetation. Prescribed fires are conducted under desired conditions to meet specific objectives, such as to restore fire regimes in adapted ecosystems or to limit the amount of dry brush in an area prone to wildfires.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1370 -->


