        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">adaptive informal economies</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">resilient informal economies, resilient informal economy, adaptive informal economy</skos:altLabel>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Measures to reduce the impacts of climate change on informal economies.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1903 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1903">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1836"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1837"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">tourism adaptation</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">climate-resilient tourism, adapting tourism, resilient tourism, adaptation of tourism, diversifying tourism, tourism diversification, adaptation of leisure travel, adaptation of tourist, adaptation of vacation, adaptation of vacations, adaptations of leisure travel, adaptations of tourism, adaptations of tourist, adaptations of vacation, adaptations of vacations, adapting leisure travel, adapting tourist, adapting vacation, adapting vacations, adaptive capacity of leisure travel, adaptive capacity of tourism, adaptive capacity of tourist, adaptive capacity of vacation, adaptive capacity of vacations, climate resilient leisure travel, climate resilient tourism, climate resilient tourist, climate resilient vacation, climate resilient vacations, climate-smart leisure travel, climate-smart tourism, climate-smart tourist, climate-smart vacation, climate-smart vacations, resilience of leisure travel, resilience of tourism, resilience of tourist, resilience of vacation, resilience of vacations, resilient leisure travel, resilient tourist, resilient vacation, resilient vacations, managing risks to tourism, managing tourism risk, managing tourism risks, resilience of the tourism, diversification of tourism, diversification of tourist</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1499"/>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Activities to reduce the impacts of climate change on tourism.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1904 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1904">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1837"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">protecting cultural and natural heritage sites</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">protecting heritage sites, protecting sacred sites, protecting cultural heritage, protecting natural heritage, protecting sites of special conservation, protecting archaeological sites, protecting national monuments, protecting world heritage, protection of archaeological sites, protection of heritage sites, protection of historical buildings, protection of national monuments, protection of natural heritage, protection of sacred sites, protection of world heritage, protecting UNESCO, protection of UNESCO</skos:altLabel>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Activities to reduce the impacts of climate change on cultural and natural heritage sites.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1905 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1905">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1830"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">strengthening institutions</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">institution strengthening, strengthening indigenous institutions, strengthening indigenous knowledge systems, empowering governance, empowering institutions, establishing strong institutions, institutional capacity building, institutions with strengthened capacity, strengthen public institutions, strengthened institutions, strengthening public institutions</skos:altLabel>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Strengthening institutions involves providing support, resources, training, and policy reforms to improve their organizational capacity, effectiveness, and ability to serve their public-serving functions, whether in government, the private sector, or civil society.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1906 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1906">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1634"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">public water conservation campaign</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">public water conservation awareness</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Public water conservation campaigns raise awareness in all levels of society about the importance of saving water to cope with its scarcity and ensure sustainability. The aim is to change citizen attitudes and behaviour to improve water use efficiency. This is done through education and awareness campaigns on the socioeconomic and environmental benefits of water conservation and different conservation methods. Communication means include traditional and social media, as well as direct communication such as workshops, presentations, stakeholder dialogues, etc. Other means such as economic incentives can also be employed, for example, free installation of water meters.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Public water conservation campaigns raise awareness in all levels of society about the importance of saving water to cope with its scarcity and ensure sustainability.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1907 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1907">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1634"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">water licensing and permits</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">water permit, water license, licenses for water use, permits for water use, water use license, water use licenses, water use permit</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Water licensing is a water demand management tool requiring private landowners, or at times specific potential water users, to apply for (or purchase) a license or permit for water-use or water affecting activities (e.g. construction, diversion, artificial recharge) in the watershed. Water licensing allows authorities and watershed managers to keep track of how much water is used, where and by whom, imposing timely restrictions where needed. Examples include mining operations in watersheds requiring water use permits, or applying for a permit to drill a borehole on private property, as well as permits to extract water for storage or irrigation.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Water licensing is a water demand management tool requiring private landowners, or at times specific potential water users, to apply for (or purchase) a license or permit for water-use or water affecting activities (e.g. construction, diversion, artificial recharge) in the watershed.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1908 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1908">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1834"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1880"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">green spaces in urban planning</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">green infrastructure, urban green space, urban green spaces, greening urban, urban tree planting, peri-urban forestry, tree planting in cities, urban forestry, green spaces in urban, parks in urban, urban greenway, urban greenways, green spaces in planning, planning green spaces, city parks, green spaces in cities, green spaces in urban planning, urban forests, urban gardens, urban parks, urban forest, urban green infrastructures, urban green area, urban green areas, urban greening, urban nature, city green spaces, municipal green infrastructure, municipal green spaces, trees in cities, urban vegetation, integration of green areas in cities, greenery in populated areas, blue-green infrastructure, green infrastructures, greenisation in urban, greenization in urban</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Green spaces are areas covered by vegetation (e.g. grass, bushes or trees), where water can permeate through the soil and vegetation, filtrating part of the sediment and pollutants before reaching the underlying groundwater. Green spaces and permeable surfaces are particularly relevant in urban settings, where they help to uptake and infiltrate water, decreasing runoff rates. The water also often contains excessive amounts of pollutants. This subsequently reduces pressure on water drainage systems and treatment facilities. The high retention capacity of vegetation makes it important for mitigating floods and managing urban storm water. Managing rainwater infiltration rates has become increasingly important to meet the challenge of increased frequency and severity of cloudbursts resulting from the changing climate. Apart from storm water management’s applicable value, green spaces also contribute to improved living environments by creating recreational areas for urban populations, contributing to air quality and creating habitats for urban biodiversity. Examples of urban green spaces include: forests, wetlands, parks, sports fields, agricultural land, gardens and green roofs. Public green spaces are protected, designed (if necessary), managed and maintained by local municipalities.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Green spaces are areas covered by vegetation (e.g. grass, bushes or trees), where water can permeate  through the soil and vegetation, filtrating part of the sediment and pollutants before reaching the  underlying groundwater. Green spaces and permeable surfaces are particularly relevant in urban  settings, where they help to uptake and infiltrate water, decreasing runoff rates.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1909 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1909">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1880"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">watershed conservation</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">watershed management, watershed protection, watershed restoration, watershed preservation, protecting water catchments, water catchment preservation, water catchment protection, water catchment restoration, management of watersheds, protection of watersheds, catchment protection</skos:altLabel>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Watershed conservation is the systematic protection, management, and restoration of all resources within a drainage basin, a geographical area where all water drains to a common outlet.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1910 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1910">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1880"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">source water protection</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">groundwater protection zones, groundwater source protection zones, protecting groundwater, groundwater source protection zone, protect groundwater, protect source water, protecting source water, water source protection</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Source water protection is a management approach used to protect public drinking water sources against contamination and overuse. This may include both surface water (rivers, lakes or streams) and underground aquifers. Identifying potential sources of contamination is an important initial step to establish sustainable management plans that ensure a long-term supply of clean water. Identifying and protecting natural sources of good water quality, such as forests, wetlands and riparian buffers, is equally important in this context. Source water protection entails management and policy measures that restrict overuse and pollution of water at its source, and may include regulations (e.g. water allocation quotas, water quality compliance regulations), compensation schemes (e.g. payments to industrial or agricultural users to reduce use of pollutants or extraction volumes, payments for ecosystem services schemes) or conservation measures in the upstream watershed. Plans are often designed in close collaboration with local communities, for example through education and awareness programs or new local governance structures.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Source water protection is a management approach used to protect public drinking water sources against contamination and overuse.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1911 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1911">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q169"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">point of use water treatment</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">domestic water treatment, boiling drinking water, domestic water filter, drinking water purification, home water filter, UV water treatment, water purifier, solar disinfection of water, biosand filtration, ceramic filtration</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Treating water at the point-of-use (such as the household) in places where centralized water treatment systems are not efficient or present can improve water quality and safety. Technological advances have resulted in a number of low-cost and effective point-of-use treatment options. These include physical filtration (e.g. using cloths, ceramics, sand or specialized filters), chlorination or disinfectant powders (a chlorine solution or disinfectant added to water, killing most bacteria and viruses), solar disinfection (water bottled in clear plastic and placed under direct sunlight – ultraviolet rays and heat kill pathogens in still water) and boiling (heat inactivates dangerous bacteria and viruses).</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Treating water at the point-of-use (such as the household) in places where centralized water treatment systems are not efficient or present can improve water quality and safety.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1912 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1912">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1636"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">water safety plan</rdfs:label>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Water safety plans are a risk management tool to ensure a safe drinking water supply for human consumption. Effective safety plans require a holistic approach to water management where all possible threats to drinking water quality in the supply chain, from catchment/supplier to consumer, are assessed and interventions to control contamination risks are enacted. A safe drinking water supply has important health benefits, and an inclusive planning approach   allows stakeholders to take responsibility while educating on the importance of safe drinking water. Rather than attempting to increase water treatment capacity, water safety plans take a preventative approach, aiming to mitigate threats before they occur. The primary objectives of water safety plans are to prevent contamination at the source, or anywhere throughout the storage and distribution system, and to treat water so that it meets predetermined quality indicators.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Water safety plans are a risk management tool to ensure a safe drinking water supply for human consumption.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1913 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1913">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q106"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">river floodplain reconnecting</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">reconnecting rivers with floodplains, river floodplain reconnection</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Reconnecting rivers with their floodplains is a green infrastructure approach that focuses on removing barriers along the edges of the river. This allows the river to re-establish its natural course over time, eventually connecting it to its historical floodplain, or creating a new one. It can include removal or setting back of levees, raising of a deeply engraved riverbed, or expanding a river’s bank. Faster solutions include manually restoring (by digging) the river close to its original form, and establish human made connections between the river and its original floodplain wetlands. Reconnecting rivers to their floodplains help to re-establish important ecological processes that deliver numerous benefits, create vital habitats for species and contribute to biodiversity, in addition to playing an important role in the provision of ecosystem services to people and nature.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Reconnecting rivers with their floodplains is a green infrastructure approach that focuses on removing barriers along the edges of the river. This allows the river to re-establish its natural course over time, eventually connecting it to its historical floodplain, or creating a new one.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1914 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1914">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2009"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">sandbags against flooding</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">flood sandbag, flood sandbags, flooding sandbag, flooding sandbags</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Sandbags are cloth bags filled with sand, and are used as a convenient way to block the movement of water and sediment. A layered structure created by stacking sandbags maintains stability and can hold back water erosion and failure of riverbanks. The protection of roads using sandbags is not a new technology, but the feature of this technology is the use of sediment, cement and jute, which is one of the strongest natural fibers, as a hardening material for the filler in the bags, with the purpose of ground reinforcement. Jute is produced in India, Bangladesh and other countries, and is also used in jute bags to hold grains; it is easy to obtain in developing countries. This is “hybrid ground technology” due to the novel combination with technology for ground improvement. This is the new method that also has improvement technique for ground.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Sandbags are cloth bags filled with sand, and are used as a convenient way to block the movement of water and sediment. A layered structure created by stacking sandbags maintains stability and can hold back water erosion and failure of riverbanks.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1916 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1916">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q106"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">floodplain zoning</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">floodplain planning, floodplain restrictions</skos:altLabel>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">A land-use planning strategy that restricts development on areas prone to river flooding to reduce flood damage and risk.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1917 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1917">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1955"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">levee</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">flood protection embankment, river embankment, flood protection embankments, river embankments</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">A man-made structure, usually an earthen embankment, designed and constructed in accordance with sound engineering practices to contain, control, or divert the flow of water so as to reduce risk from temporary flooding.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">A man-made structure, usually an earthen embankment, designed and constructed in accordance with sound engineering practices to contain, control, or divert the flow of water so as to reduce risk from temporary flooding.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1918 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1918">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1882"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">drainage canal</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">drainage system, flood drainage canal, flood drainage canals, drainage canals, drainage ditch, drainage ditches, drainage channel, drainage channels</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Agricultural drainage is carried out by systems that intercept and convey excess water across a plot and dispose of it in a safe location. The water is transported by gravity, in a non-erosive manner, in surface or sub-surface channels. The aim is to control the specific moisture content for each type of crop and avoid losses resulting from excess water in extreme situations. The size of the system depends on the depth of the water table and the maximum volume to be disposed of, but in general channels are between 0.4 and 1.5 m deep and 0.5 and 1.20 m wide. Drainage avoids crop damage from intense rain and flooding by removing excess water. It also controls soil moisture content, allowing crops to grow properly, which increases productivity and, consequently, food security. The moisture retained during the rainy season may be beneficial during the dry season.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Agricultural drainage is carried out by systems that intercept and convey excess water across a plot and dispose of it in a safe location.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1919 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1919">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1882"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">drainage gradient</rdfs:label>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Drainage gradient is a term in road design, defined as the combined slope due to road surface cross slope and longitudinal slope. If the drainage gradient is too low, rain and melt water drainage will be insufficient. This results in water pooling on the road surface, thereby increasing the risk for hydroplaning and wet-pavement vehicle crashes.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Drainage gradient is a term in road design, defined as the combined slope due to road surface cross slope and longitudinal slope.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1920 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1920">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1635"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">water pond</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">water tank, rainwater pond, rainwater ponds, water ponds, water accumulation pond, water accumulation ponds, retaining pond, retaining ponds</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Ponds are small standing waters that may be permanent or temporary, man-made or naturally created. Ponds serve as nature-based solutions that offer multiple benefits: carbon storage, water provisioning, flood control, groundwater recharge, pollution reduction, and recreation. Their high biogeochemical activity and capacity to regulate extreme weather events play an important role in climate adaptation.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Ponds are small standing waters that may be permanent or temporary, man-made or naturally created.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1922 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1922">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1884"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">riparian buffer</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">riparian buffers, establishment of riparian, expansion of riparian, restoration of riparian, restore riparian habitats, riparian habitat restoration</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Riparian buffers are vegetated, often forested, areas (“strips”) adjacent to streams, rivers, lakes and other waterways protecting aquatic environments from the impacts of surrounding land use. To the extent possible riparian buffers should compose of native species and typically are divided in three zones with a total width of 15 to 200 meters. The first zone should consist of different grasses, a middle zone planted with bushes and a last part with large trees.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">A riparian buffer is a vegetated area near a stream, usually forested, which helps shade and partially protect a stream from the impact of adjacent land uses.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1923 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1923">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1885"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">water restriction</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">hosepipe ban, water rationing</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">The amount of water available from a source can vary greatly between seasons, thus it is particularly important to ensure sustainable abstraction rates and protect water sources from over-use during dry seasons. Seasonal water rationing is an approach to control water use rates amongst different users based on the seasonal availability of water and socioeconomic priorities. Rationing may take the form of water use restrictions for certain purposes, in certain times, or certain areas. The aim is to maintain equitable use among different users, as well as high levels of water productivity, throughout the year.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Seasonal water rationing is an approach to control water use rates amongst different users based on the seasonal availability of water and socioeconomic priorities.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1924 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1924">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1887"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">disease surveillance system</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">disease surveillance</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Systems to detect disease outbreaks quickly before they spread, cost lives and become difficult to control. Effective surveillance can improve disease outbreak detection in emergency settings, such as in countries in conflict or following a natural disaster.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Systems to detect disease outbreaks quickly before they spread, cost lives and become difficult to control.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1926 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1926">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1927"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">education of health personnel</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">health personnel training, educate health staff, educate medical personnel, educate medical staff, education of health professionals, education of health staff, education of medical staff, health professionals education, health staff education, health staff training, medical personnel training, medical staff education, train health staff, train medical personnel, training health professionals, training medical personnel, training for health professionals</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Expansion and reform of health professionals’ education and training to increase the quantity, quality and relevance of health professionals, and in so doing strengthen the country health systems and improve population health outcomes.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Expansion and reform of health professionals’ education and training to increase the quantity, quality and relevance of health professionals, and in so doing strengthen the country health systems and improve population health outcomes.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1927 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1927">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1833"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">resilient public health</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">adaptive public health, resilient public health, adapting public health to climate, public health adaptation, public health resilience, adaptation of community health, adaptation of public health, adaptations of community health, adaptations of public health, adapting community health, adapting public health, adaptive capacity of community health, adaptive capacity of public health, climate resilient community health, climate resilient public health, climate-smart community health, climate-smart public health, resilience of community health, resilience of public health, resilient community health, expand public health, expanded public health, expanding public health, public health surveillance</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1571"/>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Actions to adapt public health to the impacts of climate change.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1928 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1928">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1841"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1927"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">heatwave surveillance</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">surveillance of heatwaves, surveillance for heat-related health impacts, monitoring heat impacts on health</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Monitoring the impact of heat on public health, including heat-related deaths.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Monitoring the impact of heat on public health.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1929 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1929">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1927"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">heatwave plan</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">heatwave plans, heat health action plan, heat health action plans, heat health plans, heatwave action plan, heatwave action plans</skos:altLabel>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Strategies to reduce the impacts of extreme health on human health.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1930 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1930">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1927"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">public health infrastructure</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">infrastructure for public health</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Public health infrastructure includes everything from healthcare facilities and laboratories to information systems and public health organizations.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Public health infrastructure includes everything from healthcare facilities and laboratories to information systems and public health organizations.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1931 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1931">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1888"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">vaccination against climate-sensitive disease</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">cholera vaccine, dengue vaccine, typhoid vaccine, yellow fever vaccine, malaria vaccine, immunisation against climate-sensitive disease, cholera vaccination, dengue fever vaccination, dengue fever vaccine, malaria vaccination, typhoid vaccination, yellow fever vaccination</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">animal vaccination</cpr:P9>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">animal vaccinations</cpr:P9>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">cattle vaccination</cpr:P9>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">cattle vaccinations</cpr:P9>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">livestock vaccination</cpr:P9>
        <cpr:P9 xml:lang="en">livestock vaccinations</cpr:P9>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Programs to deliver vaccines against climate-sensitive disease such as cholera, malaria and dengue fever.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1932 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1932">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1888"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">rapid diagnostic test</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">rapid diagnostic tests</skos:altLabel>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Rapid diagnostics are tools that help to quickly identify or diagnose health conditions, such as infectious diseases.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1934 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1934">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1888"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">bed net</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">bed nets, mosquito net, mosquito nets</skos:altLabel>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">A net that hangs over and around a bed to keep insects away from someone who is sleeping.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1935 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1935">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1889"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">access to nutritional food</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">improve nutrition, improved nutrition, access to nutrition, nutritional food access, improve access to healthy food, improve access to healthy foods, nutritional security</skos:altLabel>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Actions to improve and ensure access to healthy, nutritional food.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1936 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1936">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q832"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">coastal zoning</rdfs:label>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Coastal zoning is a process of delineating the coastal areas into land and sea use zones, designating permitted, prohibited and conditional uses in order to plan better management of the coastal and surrounding areas to reduce coastal vulnerability stemming from sea-level rise, changing weather patterns, storm surges and coastal erosion, which are challenging traditional systems for use and development of coastal lands. In a specific zone, certain activities can be allowed, allowed with permission, or forbidden, and the zoning can be geared to a range of different conditions, such as economic development, tourism, conservation and hazard presence. The zoning is generally managed and enforced by public authorities and can be developed in consultation with different coastal stakeholders.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Coastal zoning is a process of delineating the coastal areas into land and sea use zones, designating permitted, prohibited and conditional uses in order to plan better management of the coastal and surrounding areas to reduce coastal vulnerability stemming from sea-level rise, changing weather patterns, storm surges and coastal erosion, which are challenging traditional systems for use and development of coastal lands.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1937 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1937">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1842"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">managed realignment</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">managed coastal realignment</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Managed realignment involves setting back the line of actively maintained defences to a new line, inland of the original or preferably, to rising ground. Doing so can promote the creation of intertidal habitat between the old and new defences. Managed realignment may involve deliberate breaching or the complete removal of a current coastal defence. The process can be planned through abandonment or relocation of existing defences or unplanned through abandonment of defences if, for example, financial resources for maintaining defences are not available.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Managed realignment is the deliberate process of altering flood defences to allow flooding of a presently defended area.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1938 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1938">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2009"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">flood-proofing</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">floodproofing, flood-resistant structures, flood-proof structure, flood-proof structures, flood-resistant structure</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">The primary objective of flood-proofing is to reduce or avoid the impacts of coastal flooding upon structures. This may include elevating structures above the floodplain, employing designs and building materials which make structures more resilient to flood damage and preventing floodwaters from entering structures in the flood zone, amongst other measures.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">The primary objective of flood-proofing is to reduce or avoid the impacts of flooding upon structures.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1939 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1939">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1891"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q2794"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">coastal wetland restoration</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">coastal wetland rehabilitation, restoring coastal wetlands, tidal wetland rehabilitation, tidal wetland restoration, coastal wetlands restoration, restoration of coastal wetlands, restore coastal wetland, restore coastal wetlands, restored coastal wetlands, rehabilitation of coastal wetland</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">The term ‘wetland’ refers to a diverse range of shallow water and intertidal habitats, which occur in various locations around the world.  Wetland restoration relates to the rehabilitation of previously existing wetland functions from a more impaired to a less impaired or unimpaired state of overall function. Although similar to managed realignment, wetland restoration can be distinguished by the goal to maintain the present position of the coastline as opposed to realigning landward, as occurs under managed realignment.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">The term ‘wetland’ refers to a diverse range of shallow water and intertidal habitats, which occur in various locations around the world.  Wetland restoration relates to the rehabilitation of previously existing wetland functions from a more impaired to a less impaired or unimpaired state of overall function.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1940 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1940">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1891"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">coral reef restoration</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">restoration of coral reefs, improve reef health, coral reef rehabilitation, coral reef restored, coral reefs rehabilitated, coral reefs restoration, coral reefs restored, rehabilitate coral, rehabilitated coral, rehabilitating coral, rehabilitation of coral, restoration of coral, restoration of corals, restore coral, restored coral, restoring coral, coral rehabilitation, coral restoration, restore degraded coral, restore degraded reef, coral transplantation, coral transplanting, transplantation of corals, transplanting corals</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P3 rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q435"/>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Actions to protect and improve the function of coral reefs.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1941 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1941">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1891"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">mangrove restoration</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">restoration of mangroves, mangrove rehabilitation, rehabilitation of mangroves, rehabilitation of mangrove, replanting mangrove, replanting mangroves, restoration of mangrove, restoring mangrove, restoring mangroves, mangrove planting, planting mangrove, planting mangroves, restore mangrove, restore mangroves, mangrove areas restored, mangrove rehabilitated, mangrove restored, mangroves rehabilitated, mangroves rehabilitation, mangroves restored, rehabilitate mangrove, rehabilitate mangroves, rehabilitated mangrove, rehabilitated mangroves, rehabilitation of degraded mangrove, rehabilitation of degraded mangroves, restoration of degraded mangrove, restoration of degraded mangroves, restore degraded mangrove, restore degraded mangroves, restored mangrove, restored mangroves, afforestation of mangroves, mangrove afforestation, mangroves replanted, planted mangrove, planted mangroves, replant mangrove, replant mangroves, replanted mangroves, mangroves are restored</skos:altLabel>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Actions to protect and improve the function of mangrove forests.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1942 -->


    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1942">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://climatepolicyradar.wikibase.cloud/entity/Q1891"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">seagrass bed management</rdfs:label>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">management of seagrass beds, management of seagrass meadows, seagrass bed protection, seagrass meadow management, seagrass meadow protection, management of seagrass, conservation of seagrass, conserving seagrass, manage seagrass, protect seagrass, protecting seagrass, protection of seagrass, rehabilitation of seagrass, restoration of seagrass, restore seagrass, restoring seagrass, seagrass bed conservation, seagrass bed restoration, seagrass beds protected, seagrass beds restored, seagrass conservation, seagrass management, seagrass meadow restoration, seagrass meadows conservation, seagrass meadows managed, seagrass meadows protected, seagrass meadows restored</skos:altLabel>
        <cpr:P7 xml:lang="en">Seagrasses are submerged marine flowering plants forming extensive meadows in many shallow coastal waters worldwide. The leafy shoots of these highly productive plants provide food and shelter for many animals (including commercially important species, e.g. prawns), and their roots and rhizomes are important for oxygenating and stabilising bottom sediments and preventing erosion. Seagrass bed management is protecting seagrass meadows from sediment loading and disturbances caused by storms, eutrophication, temperature increases and other light restrictions due to sea-level rise and increased cloudiness.</cpr:P7>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Protecting and managing seagrass beds.</schema:description>
    </owl:Class>
    


